>Owen Peter Vajk wrote:

>> This is a bit of a random question, but are there any video capture
>> cards that are supported under Linux?

>I dunno about the videocards, it's probably the best thing to install NT
>(I know it sucks, but hey, you can't have everything) for video capture
>and get something like the DPS Hollywood (if you can afford it :-)).

Also, depending on the amount of video you want to capture, you could
consider trying the BT848 driver coupled with a two fairly fast disks.
At 768x576, each frame is "only" 880k. With 25fps, you get 22M/s.
If you distribute the data stream between two disks, and put nice big
FIFOs in front of each to buffer recalibration and the like, you should
be able to capture full quality, full framerate.
Of course, on an Alpha you might also be able to do some high-quality 
compression in realtime and thus reduce the data rate tremendously.
There is a package called RTjpeg, which tries something of that sort
for much more modest hardware ;-)

