On Tue, 24 Nov 1998, Owen Peter Vajk wrote:

> This is a bit of a random question, but are there any video capture
> cards that are supported under Linux?  I'm posting to this goup because
> the machine I would probably end up installing it on is an apha 533
> running redhat 5.1.

try http://pcisys.net/~vleo/linuxvideo.html

The drivers are open source, although I don't know if they'll compile
out-of-the-box on an Alpha. And it's still under some development, both
the hardware and drivers.

The card does on-board Motion-JPEG  compression. i.e compress every frame
using JPEG; not as good as MPEG but easier in hardware. It can do full
NTSC (720x480 @30 fps) or PAL (720x576 @25 fps) resolution, with
compression ratios ranging from 3.5:1 to 30:1 (i.e about 8.5M/s to
0.99M/s, respectively).


Also look at the Video4Linux kernel subsystem in the latest development
kernels.

	http://roadrunner.swansea.uk.linux.org/v4l.shtml

It supports many frame-grabber type cards. Seeing as how it's now part of
the official kernel release (and has been for some time), and that stable
kernel version 2.2 is just around the corner, I imagine these drivers
would be fairly cross-platform.

The 2.2 kernel should be released by christmas. Put one of these cards on
your christmas list and you should have a VERY merry christmas! ;)

hope this helps.
bye

