Hello all,

I am sure that there are docs somewhere on how to do this, but I cannot find
them.  If there is a archive site or some place else these questions are 
addressed, point me the way and I will politely RTFM.  I have concatenated
three problems in this email, suspecting that they may be somewhat related.

This is an   Alpha LX164 running RedHat 5.0.   
 

Kernel upgrade:

I am trying to upgrade my kernel, and everything seems to work until I try to
reboot.  Ok, first what versions of requisite files do I have installed:

modutils 2.1.55 (to go to 2.1.121, it has to be compiled under a 2.1.x kernel
			according to its documentation)
binutils 2.1.55   
gcc	 2.7.2.3
glibc    2.0.7
ld.so    2.0.7
g++lib   2.7.2.8
procps   1.2.9
procinfo 14 (1998-5-24)
psmisc   17
mount    2.7f  (see note below)
sh-utils 1.16
bash 	 1.14.7(1)

I use "make boot" to create the new kernel, and it builds a new vmlinux.gz
which I copy to the / directory. The problem occurs when I reboot. MILO 
executes the kernel just fine until after it mounts the root directory. 
I get the following message before the system halts (keyboard is still able to
do ctrl-alt-del to reboot):

VFS: Mounted root (ext2 filesystem) readonly
Freeing unused kernel memory: 120K freed
Warning: unable to open an initial console
Kernel Panic: No init found. Try passing init= option to kernel.
 
Where is docs on kernel options?; I saw nothing in Kernel-HOWTO. Is it looking
for a path to init? It is in /sbin/init. Is it looking for a runlevel? 
Currently, I am only sending the boot option (boot=sda2/vmlinux.gz).

If I reboot using my old kernel, everything goes fine.  I have read the MILO
FAQ/documentation mentioned online at gatekeeper.dec.com, and nothing looks
suspicious on this end.  I tried to compile MILO from sources, but it fails
(details available if requested).  The docs state that MILO version does not
have to match kernel version.

Oh, I am trying to upgrade to kernel 1.2.129 from 2.0.32(p). 

---------

Compiling mount.  The new kernel wants mount 2.7l.  Well, I pulled the SRPMS 
mount-2.8a from a redhat ftp mirror (no compiled rpms for the Alpha yet) and
it will not compile.  I get the following error:

gcc -c -Wall -Wstrict-prototypes -Wmissing-prototypes -O2 -DHAVE_NFS swapon.c
In file included from swapon.c:13:
swap.h:13: `PAGE_SIZE' undeclared here (not in a function)
swap.h:13: size of array `reserved' has non-integer type
swapon.c: In function `swap':
swapon.c:98: warning: implicit declaration of function `swapon'
swapon.c:101: warning: implicit declaration of function `swapoff'
make: *** [swapon.o] Error 1

When swap.configure is run, it reports

Your libc thinks that swapon has 1 arg only.
Define SWAPON_NEEDS_TWO_ARGS in swapon.c if you want to use priorities.

When I set that define in swapon.c, make reports

gcc -c -Wall -Wstrict-prototypes -Wmissing-prototypes -O2 -DHAVE_NFS swapon.c
In file included from swapon.c:13:
swap.h:13: `PAGE_SIZE' undeclared here (not in a function)
swap.h:13: size of array `reserved' has non-integer type
swapon.c:67: parse error before `swapon'
swapon.c:67: warning: function declaration isn't a prototype
swapon.c:69: parse error before `swapoff'
swapon.c:69: warning: function declaration isn't a prototype
swapon.c: In function `swap':
swapon.c:96: warning: implicit declaration of function `swapon'
swapon.c:101: warning: implicit declaration of function `swapoff'
swapon.c: At top level:
swapon.c:67: warning: `_syscall2' declared `static' but never defined
swapon.c:69: warning: `_syscall1' declared `static' but never defined
make: *** [swapon.o] Error 1


The swapon and swapoff functions are listed in the man(2) pages.  However, I
could not find their delcarations in the header files unistd.h or in 
linux/swap.h. I had installed  glibc-2.0.7-29.alpha.src.rpm again from the
ftp mirror site (again no problems). I used rpm to verify that the headers
were correct for the glibc version, and they were. Suggestions as to what is 
going on? 
 
============

Calls to setresgid function not working (may be related to above problem).
While compiling samba (I need its PDC support), I get the following error:

Linking bin/smbd
smbd/chgpasswd.o: In function `dochild':
chgpasswd.c(.text+0x710): undefined reference to `setresgid'
chgpasswd.c(.text+0x714): undefined reference to `setresgid'
chgpasswd.c(.text+0x748): undefined reference to `setresgid'
chgpasswd.c(.text+0x74c): undefined reference to `setresgid'
smbd/uid.o: In function `init_uid':
uid.c(.text+0x74): undefined reference to `setresgid'
smbd/uid.o(.text+0x78):uid.c: more undefined references to `setresgid' follow
make: *** [bin/smbd] Error 1


Note that all of the necessary object files were created just fine, which
to me indicates that the headers are ok but the requisite libraries cannot
be found. I have seen postings from people who are running this software
on RedHat 5.1 (albeit, probably on Intel based systems), so I would think
that this problem can be solved.

 


