Olaf Schnapauff wrote:
> 
> Jack W. Crenshaw <jcrens@magicnet.net> wrote:
> : Peter Mardahl wrote:
> :>
> :> Alpha doesn't include any trig functions, nor log, nor exp, but as
> :> of 21264 it includes sqrt.
> :>
> 
> : Hm.  That puts it at a significant disadvantage, performance-wise, from
> : the Pentium chip which has all the transcendental functions in the FPU.
> 
> No it doesnt. The idea of RISC cpus is to have little in hardware, to
> make those functions it has FAST and the pipelines more effective.
> 
> A CISC cpu like the Intel x86 has more functions in hardware, including
> some that are rarely used, which makes all functions slower (in priciple,
> as usual the truth is a little more complex).
> 
> (Check out the book of Hennesy/Patterson for more)
> 
> Olaf
> 
> --

Well, the thing is, the C [substitute your favorite language here]
compiler has to have a math library with subroutines to calculate the
trig functions.  I find it hard to believe that one could write a
subroutine, with calling list (admittedly, only one argument) and call &
return mechanism, that could compete with the Pentium math coprocessor.

Then again, maybe one could.  As I recall, the Microsoft compiler, at
least, does a lot of stuff in software before and after invoking the
FPU.

Jack



