Subject: Re: Alpha's floating point performance
Date: 1998/11/19
Message-ID: <7325lu$554$1@news.tuwien.ac.at>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 413656197
References: <3643A2A1.DECD63A@cs.sunysb.edu> <3653768C.FD9D4815@west.net> <72vvv9$kac$1@tesla.EECS.Berkeley.EDU> <Pine.LNX.4.03.9811191621290.22123-100000@callisto.stone.oz.au> <731iaq$lsc$1@tesla.EECS.Berkeley.EDU> <rz7lnl7effx.fsf@corton.inria.fr>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Vienna University of Technology, Austria
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

In article <rz7lnl7effx.fsf@corton.inria.fr>,
	Robert Harley <harley@corton.inria.fr> writes:
> 
> peterm@tesla.EECS.Berkeley.EDU (Peter Mardahl) writes:
>>Alpha has hardware instructions for dealing with single precision.
>>I think you're right in that the same execution units are used for all
>>precisions, but I suspect that the execution units are organized in
>>such a way that either there is no conversion needed, or conversion is
>>so fast that the ops are no slower.
> 
> That's about it.  Single-precision numbers are "expanded" into
> numerically equivalent double-precision ones in the floating-point
> registers.  Each f.-p. operation specifies whether the result is to be
> single or double precision, which affects rounding, exponent checking
> etc.
> 
> I'm not quite sure what happens if you ask for a single precision
> result operating on double-precision quantities that are not equal to
> expanded single-precision ones, though.
> 

The Alpha manual tells:

Single-precision operations applied to canonical single-precision values
give single-precision results. Single-precision operations applied to
non-canonical operands give UNPREDICTABLE results.

No conversion from single to double is needed, since both use the same
representation in a register. A double has to be converted to a single
and this instruction (CVTTS) can throw exeptions.

Single and double adds/muls have the same latency (4 cycles 164, 6 cycles
on the 064). The latency for a div is different (064: single 30/34, double
59/63; 164: single 15-31, double 22-60). If you use singles instead of
doubles you can reduce cache trashing.

Andi

P.S: I hate all the floating point stuff, especially if you have to
implement JVM compatible IEEE arithmetic :-).
