<gallun@my-dejanews.com> wrote: 
>I just got  a Dec 3000 which I am trying to bring on line....
>
>I was not provided with the root password and don't quite know how to
>go about getting into the system. It is my first experience with OSF.
>
>Does anyone know what filesystem this uses?

It should indicate an OSF/1 version number; if it's "old," then it is
highly probable that it is using "UFS," which is a fairly generic
"official UNIX" file system.

Alternatively, it may be using Digital's "AdvFS" Advanced Filesystem.
Which is quite cool, in that it supports such things as:
- Striping
- Assigning multiple partitions to multiple "virtual" filesystems
- Journalling, hence no need to "fsck"

Linux doesn't have anything quite like AdvFS yet.

>Do I have any hope of mounting the FS under Linux so I can read/modify the
>password file???

Not hardly.  If you've got a bootable CD (quite possible), you may be
able to boot from CD and get root access without the system consulting
/etc/passwd on the "true" root partition.

I've not had opportunity to need to do that, so I unfortunately can't
offer counsel on how to do it. 

Note that the 3000 series uses TurboChannel, and Linux doesn't grok
anything other than PCI on Alphas.  You're not likely to be running
Linux on it... 

