I recently put together an Alpha system, I began with no knowledge of
Alpha hardware.  I ended up with a a 21164LX based system.  Here's what I
can tell you:

On Sun, 22 Nov 1998, Andrei A. Dergatchev wrote:
> So far, I've talked to
>  o Microway
>  o Aspen Systems
>  o DCG
> All three have Alpha workstations based on the 21164LX, in the
> $2200-2500 price range.
I spoke with all three of these companies.  Price was my foremost
concern, so I ended up buying my motherboard from DCG because it saved me
a few hundred bucks.  I would however highly recommend Aspen.  Their
customer service was excellent and responsive.  Having a limited knowledge
of the system, you would probably benefit most from a sales/support staff
that is a as helpful as Aspen's.

> Could someone experienced shed light on what is a difference between
> Alpha 21164, Alpha 21164A and Alpha 21164PC ? Is this difference a
> reason for about $1K difference in price between models with 21164A and
> 21164PC from Microway or are some (unknown to me) LX and SX
> modifications being involved ?
The 21164A is a slightly newer model of the 21164.  Details are available
on the Digital Alpha OEM site, which you can find from Digital's main page
at www.digital.com.  The 21164PC is cheaper because it has half the cache.
It comes with 1MB of cache, while the other chips come with 2MB.  It is an
attempt to provide a cheaper Alpha, it comes at a cost in performance.
I've heard as much as a 40% loss in performance.  The PC164LX motherboard
has a 21164 processor, and *I believe* that the PC164SX has a 21164PC.
Don't quote me on that.  You'll also find a full description of these
motherboards on the Alpha OEM site.

I run Linux on my system, and am quite happy with it.  The 164LX
motherboard is a good choice for linux, because it is common and well
supported.  I've a had a little bit of a hard time finding sufficient
documentation fully explaining the current state of Linux support for
Alpha, but there do seem to be some minor issues with patches and library 
compatibilities. I didn't buy a pre-built, pre-configured system, so I
haven't had the advantage of tech. support to help me figure some of these
things out.

				Hope that helps.




