Note that an Intel Xeon running at 450MHz is pretty likely to be
comparable in horsepower to a Cray 1.  Yesteryear's supercomputer can
become tomorrow's desktop machine. 

A Compaq Alpha CPU does not require significantly more silicon than an
Intel Pentium II, hence it makes some sense to beg the question:

"Why *should* it be more expensive?"

If the plans at AMD to use the EVP bus and "Socket 8" for their K7
processor work out well, along with the plans for future Alphas to be
Socket 8-compatible, the pricing of Alpha-based systems may very well
drop to "cheap enough" levels. 

If Intel deploys Celeron 600A systems next year for about $1000, what
forcible reason *should* prevent the deployment of Alpha 633 systems at
that time for similar pricing?  Note that the Alpha CPU is likely to
require fewer transistors.
