>
>Could it be he meant the 21164 ???????

Maybe he DID mean the 21164.  But he'd still be wrong:
even the 21164PC in .25u is 1/2 the area (not 1/4) of a .25u P-II.
(I scaled the .35u 164PC chip to .25u.)

>> Your point on scale of production is correct, I think, but maufacturing
>> cost isn't likely to be cheaper.  Of course, it performs 2x as well, so
>> paying more for it is justified.
>
>
>This looks like it really MEANS the 21164 !!??? 21264 with a decent
>compiler resp. good subroutines, should be more like 4-5 x P II 450 MHz.

No, I said that, and it's true for integer operations, at least at the moment.
Look at:
http://www.specbench.org/osg/cpu95/results/res98q3/

You will see:
Processor	SPECint95	SPECfp95
575MHz 21264	30.3		47.7	 (DEC 8400)
450MHz P-II	17.6		13.8	 (Dell for int, Intel for fp)
767MHz 21164A	23.3		25.2	 (Kryotech)

The 21264 delivers less than 2x perf. in integer, and 3.5x perf
in FP.  The 21164 is only mildly faster in int, and 2x perf in FP.

PeterM



