Hi,

I use E, and it works just fine.  No need to uninstall rpms, E will find
the libraries just fine.  I use
./configure --prefix=/usr
when installing .tar.gzs to make sure everything is in the same place,
and that everybody can link to it all (yes I know I can add
/usr/local/lib to /etc/ld.so.conf, but don't want to).

AFAIK, the /usr vs. /usr/local issue is the only one that makes mixing
rpms and .tar.gzs problematic.  Put everything in /usr and it should all
work fine.  That's what I do and it works flawlessly (though I haven't
installed any RPMs since installing RH 5.1)- though I have never been
able to get esd (sound daemon) to work properly on alpha.  There was
some 64-bit uncleanness which got cleaned up around a month ago, but
esdcat still plays .wav files about 5 times too fast.

Yes, E's screen shots are awesome, and it lives up to them.  I started
using it when DR14 came out, so I never dealt with it in its former
memory/CPU-intensive state.  It is quite lightweight and fast, and did I
mention it looks awesome?  If you can stomach it, it's worth getting the
latest CVS version (cool new teme, supports iconification, docking,
shading, GNOME root menu), or a snapshot.  And be sure to get Eterm and
play with the transparent backgrounds- with shading and tinting.  Did I
mention its good looks?

Have fun!

