Compaq's Alpha chips (formerly Digital) is a potential goldmine if Compaq
knew what to do with them.

Now that Linux has taken-off there is huge potential market for its Alpha
chips if Compaq were bright enough to recognize this. If it were to start
thinking of marketing the Alpha chips as commodity items, it could get a
firm foothold and a leg-up on Intel in the 64 bit market. It could make a
killing by selling its the Alpha's and matching I/O chipsets to
manufacturers of mother boards the way Intel does. I am certain that there
would be a big demand for competitively priced Alpha systems by Linux power
users intent on turning them into servers and workstations. It would even be
better if the motherboard makers made SMP Alpha mother boards. I would
certainly like to get my hands on an Alpha based system if it were cheap
enough.

If compaq wants a part of the market it had better move soon before Intel
brings out its big gun, the 64-bit monster. Linux user's should actively
encourage Compaq to make the move towards commodification of Alpha chips. It
is in the interest of Linux users that there is  healthy competition between
CPU manufacturers. From Compaq's view, there is potention for great profits
on Compaq's side.

Linux users and Compaq could begin by making an Linux/Alpha hardware
platform specification. Given such a specification, mother board
manufactures could turn out their motherboards in the millions, and Linux
hackers could hack away at making the best OS ever without incurring the
current high cost of purchasing Alpha systems and in turn, feed the frenzy
for Alpha chips. The internet and every server on it could very well be run
by Alpha computers -- all to Compaq's benefit, if it weren't so blind. When
I visited Compaq's site they had information about Alpha geared towards the
NT crowd, but there was no mention of Linux. Man they are stupid! If I were
Compaq's CEO, I'd know what to do!!

(By the way I know that Compaq has Alpha boards containing bios designed
with NT in mind. Are such boards the platform for Linux alpha systems?)

Is there any organized effort out there in Linux land, lobbying and pointing
Compaq towards what is clearly in its best interest? Where are you,  you
wizards of Linux marketing? We have another task for you! I want to see
Linus Torvalds at a press conference sitting with the Compaq CEO and
swaggering: "When the 128-bit Alpha processor comes out, I can guarantee you
that in two months we will have Linux running on it."

Long live Linux/Alpha !!!!


Edward








