  FWIW, I spoke with a Compaq representive at ESC-West last week 
(where they had a dual-proc 21264 running QuakeII sans any video 
acceleration for a demo -- WOOF, that thing flew at 1280x1024!), 
and he claimed to have three Linux developers in the office whose
job it is to make sure Linux runs on the new systems.

  I don't think Compaq is blind to the importance of Linux.

  Sheer speculation on my part:  Compaq's first chance for a bite
out of Intel's marketshare will come with the 21164PC's process
shrink.  This *should* bring the price down to <$200 and raise the
clock rate significantly.  Motherboards for the 21164PC are already
available and relatively inexpensive (which will not be true of the
21272-based motherboards for a while), which is important.
  The hard part will be getting enough software vendors on the bus.
Does anyone know if ApplixWare and WordPerfect are available for 
AlphaLinux?  Cheap Alpha + Linux + KDE + Enlightenment + ApplixWare
+ WordPerfect would be a recipe for success, especially if someone
put it all in the same package and pitched it to the business sector
along with a comprehensive support program.  Businesses like single-
stop solutions.

  -- TTK




