Doesn't fit with my 'benchmarking'.
Which goes as follows:
Same code, same compiler, the 533 Mhz Linux alpha
is about three times faster than a Pentium II 300 Mhz.
That would be about two times faster than a 450 Mhz Pentium II.
But this is both EGCS.
I've seen similiar results in this thread, so maybe it's not too bad
a test. A DU Compiler will give you some 10-30 % speed increase,
depending on how much you help the compiler, (memory access
being the main factor here.)
If OTOH you actually do matrix manipulation
with real large, dense matrices you might see the Linux alpha
outrunning the DU box.
The latter is  entirely due to Goto's incredible
assembler routines. But since those routines
constitute the very heart of LAPACK,
(Lapack sit's atop of the BLAS), this result
carries over to most of LAPACK's other routines.

Christian




