I agree.  As I see it, Alpha has two advantages over the forthcoming IA-64:

     1.  Alpha is likely to be a faster platform in the early days of IA-64.

     2.  Alpha is a mature technology.  Many bugs have been shaken out, and
         there is software presently available for it.

Just as important, there is no reason why most x86 programmers should find it
easier to port to IA-64 than to Alpha.  Both will require rethinking in 64
bits.  Both probably will support Windows NT and Linux (Alpha for sure).  One
advantage the IA-64 will have is Solaris.

