Vinhthuy Phan wrote:

> So do you think that in general a 533Mhz apha is roughly twice as fast as
> a 450Mhz Pentium II, both using EGCS?

Unfortunately there is no such things as roughly ;-)
Fortunately though the above is true for *our* code.
Or to put it this way: I wouldn't exchange my 533 Mhz alpha
for two 450 Mhz Pentiums.
(We have both, a 300 Mhz Pentium II and a 533 Mhz Alpha,
so my judgement is based on that)
The thing is this:
If you take a little bit care of the actual architecture of the alpha,
you get a speed increase by  a factor two or even more.
Example:
You code a general  matrix-multiplication (gemm)
You do this in a 'regular` way and you end up with
a throughput of 60-80 Mflops (all egcs, large matrices)
Now you do cache-blocking, loop unrolling and you end up
with  300-500 Mflops.
(DU compiler usually reaches that region already in step one,
egcs doesn't, again: depends on the code )
The extreme situation: You code this thing in assembler and get
something like 800-900 Mflops. (independent of the size of
the matrices, Goto's gemm)
If you'd do the same with a Pentium II 450 Mhz you'd *also* get
some 60-80 Mflops in the first step or maybe even *more*.
The performance of the alpha (and the pentium) is totally
swamped by access to memory.

> I'm trying to find out as much about the difference between 450Mhz
> Pentium II and Alpha systems running linux as possible.  Any comment on
> this will be greatly appreciated.

It's actually very easy:
If you can manage to hold you data/instructions in the cache,
get an alpha, the alpha is capable of reaching an incredible throughput.
How much you *eventually* get, really depends on your code.
And that can differ between 'same performance/slower' and
'10 times faster'. (being unfair here .-))

Christian
P.S Our code isn't assembler.
P.P.S get an Alpha anyway, the prizes don't differ that much.
P.P.P.S What's the state on Digital porting their compilers to Linux ?










