Subject: Blocking SIGFPEs?
Date: 1998/11/13
Message-ID: <364C01F6.709F7471@art.co.uk>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 411404013
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
X-NNTP-Posting-Host: threebti.demon.co.uk:158.152.73.32
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
X-Complaints-To: abuse@demon.net
X-Trace: news.demon.co.uk 910950876 nnrp-11:12116 NO-IDENT threebti.demon.co.uk:158.152.73.32
Organization: Advanced Rendering Technology
MIME-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

It appears that with the variant of the 2.0.35 kernel used in Red Hat
5.2 it is not possible to block SIGFPEs using sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK,
...).

Although SIGFPE is correctly marked for blocking, at the three places in
arch/alpha/kernel/traps.c where the signal is raised the following code
is used:

    force_sig(SIGFPE, current);

Using force_sig causes the signal to be unblocked and the SIGFPE is then
delivered immediately.

SIGFPEs can be successfully blocked in this manner on Digital UNIX
V4.0B.

My quick fix was to replace the three force_sig calls with the
following:

    send_sig(SIGFPE, current, 1);

This appears to do the trick but I would much appreciate comments from
those more familiar with this area of the alpha linux kernel!  I suspect
there may have been a good reason for using force_sig in preference to
send_sig.

Thanks in anticipation
--
John Connett (jrc@art.co.uk)



