Bram Stolk wrote:
> Doug Chapman USG wrote:
> > As far as I know the size is the only disadvantage.  With 128MB of
> > ram you shouldn't see any real difference.  The only time I would
> > think it would be an issue is with 8MB or so.
> 
> Are unused portions of the kernel eligable to be swapped
> to disk?
> 
> Or can only app data be swapped out?
> 
> If the former is true, then even on a 8MB machine, a larger
> kernel is not much of a disadvantage.


I do not think that parts of the kernel itself can be swaped out.
Some dynamically allocated memory in the kernel should be eligible
for swapping.

The reason you get a memory savings with modules is that when you
are not using a device the module gets unloaded.  For example, if
you use ppp you can compile the ppp support and the serial port
driver all as modules.  A few minutes after you disconnect those
modules will be automatically unloaded.

- Doug



