	BA<bpa-shu@excite.co.uk> writes:
> Hi, just wanting some information. We are considering installing an Alpha
> 533Mhz system running redhat linux with a 18Gb SCSI hard disk to act as our
> group file server. We'd like the Alpha to export it's home directory so that
> we can nfs mount it on other Sun Solaris boxes and Intel linux boxes. Does
> anybody have any experience on how the Alpha performs on this? 


We use Alphas (under DU) as fileservers, and as expected, they rock.
Boxes like the NetApps (which also rock) are just Alphas with lots of
SCSI/FC and a dedicated OS.

However, if Linux's NFS implementation is still incomplete (which
everybody who didn't license it from Sun is subject to) then I wouldn't
really recommend it.

Another site I was at had a lot of problems with a Linux box serving up
NFS to Sun machines - we'd regularly get NFS hangs and related crashes,
and periodically file corruption. I eventually convinced them to move
the NFS responsibility to a Sun. Now that NFSv3 is out and more systems
properly do NFS over TCP (about damn time, too) this may no longer be
an issue.

> We haven't used Alphas before, so how well supported are Alphas? Would we be
> better off using a PII 450Mhz system instead for the task? For your reference

You'd be better off making sure Linux can do the NFS work you want.
Linux runs great on the Alpha. Whether or not it is a cost-effective
choice for something as dinky as serving up one disk is up to you.
(Off hand, we have about 30 hanging off one machine and it could easily
take two or three times that - we'd run out of PCI slots before we hit
a system bus or computational limit.) Heck, for a one disk NFS box, we
have a SPARCstation 1 :>


