> Actually, aside from Goto's gemm (which is cheating because it's hand-optimized
> assembler, but relevant because I use it every day :-), I have never heard of a
> single case where Linux/egcs beat NT/VC++ (whose internals were written by DEC).

I know a case where alpha gcc beat alpha VC++, but the difference was
only
about 10-15%.  With the version of alpha VC++ we used for the benchmark,
there was no way to *really* force register variables (you can declare
them, but the compiler ignores the declarations).  In gcc, the
register declarations made a significant difference, since the
time-critical
function had about 16 integer and 20 float vars that were used
repeatedly.



