> >> motherboard design.
>
> > They are full of shit.  In particular, the AIM guys are famous for
> > extrapolating from synthetic or component benchmarks to bad conclusions.
>
> ... and they used WinNT as OS, which I was told is the slowest of all OSes
> for Alphas (it can't even do 64 bit?).

Actually, aside from Goto's gemm (which is cheating because it's hand-optimized
assembler, but relevant because I use it every day :-), I have never heard of a
single case where Linux/egcs beat NT/VC++ (whose internals were written by DEC).

One CalTech professor wrote to ask how to improve the performance of egcs 1.1
because VC++ was beating it, and we couldn't get egcs any better than 30% slower
on his alpha system.  Since speed is of paramount importance to him, he chose to
use NT, though he would rather have used Unix (and didn't want to pay through
the nose for a 2-user DU license).

Unix has a TON of advantages over NT, which is why I use Linux; but unless you
have a specific benchmark that proves otherwise, please don't claim that
Linux/egcs produces faster binaries than NT/VC++.  It simply isn't true, and
hurts the credibility of the Linux community.

[Related question: has anyone tested NT vs. Linux in network or disk
performance?  I would guess Linux is faster in these areas...]

