My impression is that you're right on both counts about why egcs is slower: manpower
for the technical challenge (most of the egcs developers are probably working on
Pentium; optimizing for alpha requires detailed knowledge about how the processor
and cache hierarchy work), and DEC holding out on us.

However, think about the economics again: when Samsung/Intel sells a $700 21164A,
DEC probably gets a small royalty- especially since their chip business used to lose
money.  When they sell Dec Unix ($1700 for a "two-user license") and their
outrageously priced compilers (I think I've heard $1500 for C/C++/fortran), they
make a TON of money!  I'm not sure, but I'd believe even the royalties from the M$
Visual C++ suite are higher than for the processors/boards.  If someone wants the
faster compilers, might as well make them pay extra for the OS and compiler suite,
rather than just making a few bucks for each processor- in DEC's view, that is.
Same goes for network and other I/O performance, which I've heard are significantly
better for DU than Linux-alpha.

Furthermore, corporate types still believe that mail list/newsgroup support is
inferior to having a solid company like DEC/Compaq stand behind their products, and
are willing to shell out big bucks for this "privilege".

It would take a lot of effort to convince them that a better egcs for alpha would
lead to the vastly higher alpha sales they'd need to justify letting that knowledge
out of the bag.

