update flash!  my 4/233 arrived from webauction.
I promptly removed the 2 x 8mb SIMMs and tried some ECC memory.
NO GO -- The system did not recognize it at all.
Then I tried some regular PC-style 4x36 parity memory; its good!
I've got three banks of 32Mb.

I "threw" in a 2Gb SCSI disk and a not-so-new 2x SCSI CD-ROM drive,
and a 4Mb Matrox Millenium card (not Millenium-II).
I must say the case and chassis are well made; much nicer
electrical shielding than the average non-server PC.

My machine came pre-configured with the SRM console,
it did not come with any graphics card.

The next thing I did was to update the BIOS using the
DOS "mkboot" from the DEC ftp site.

Now, EVERYONE PLEASE EXCUSE WHAT I DID NEXT:
I loaded the ARC console and installed Windows NT 4 Workstation,
applied Service Pack 3, Installed Visual C++ 5.0 for RISC, and
generally trashed the machine about.  Basically I wanted to 
see if I would encounter any system problems (like memory errors.)
I'm happy to report that everything worked well.  I would        
subjectively judge the speed of the 4/233 running NT to be
faster than the pentium 133 donor machine (where I got the
video card from), and slower than a Pentium Pro 180 machine.
This is by no means quantitative, just me doing a "make all"
compile with MSVC 5.0 (MSVC 6.0 on the PP180) on 30k lines
of code, and since the other machines have ultra (40mhz)
adaptec controllers, and the 4/233 has a 10Mhz controller,
I'd say this " $300 " machine made a pretty good showing.

Now I just have to be patient because
(1) I'm waiting for my Redhat 5.2 CD-ROM
and
(2) I spent way too much time "playing" when I have work and
    a big school programming project to get done.

Meanwhile, I have a folder of documents from all those great
Alpha-Linux web sites that I need to read thru, but at least
I feel (that on some superficial level) my hardware works.

best wishes,

Alex




