>>For most computations 1/0 represents a severe error
>but I would beg to differ. For trig and algebra, 1/0 is a linear
>infinity, and (like with eg superstrings) you don't give a damn about
>infinity provided at the end they all cancel out and give you your
>answer. Which for trig and algebra you know they will.

If you're planning to handle divergencies properly I suggest you need to be
_very_ clear about what sort of divergencies you're dealing with and do the
necessary analysis before programming it. I'm unaware of a C library that
allows this sort of analysis of limits.

I have no idea what you mean when you say 1/0 is a 'linear infinity'. 1/0 is
undefined - full stop. You are entitled to talk about the
_limit_ of 1/x or 1/x^2 as x tends to 0. You may be able to get a finite
answer from some combination of functions that are divergent at some point.

Generally an infinity arising in a mathematical model is a sign of
deficiency in the model.

>I agree that when you start dividing different degrees of infinity by
>each other things start getting complicated ... It's just a case of
>knowing your infinities (yes there are an INFINITE NUMBER of them, I
>believe :-)

I suggest you read a book on analysis.

regards

Stefan

