Organization: AT&T WorldNet Services
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

The Alpha is much better platform to do development on. If you tinker with
assembly on
both platforms you will quickly see the potential, albeit mostly untapped,
of the Alpha.
The Alpha is a true WORKSTATION, the Intel is a Home PC. The biggest
difference amoung the
architectures is the number of registers. the Alpha has 32 Integer and 32
FPU registers. When
programming on the Intel you constantly do stores and pops because you only
have 8 registers.
Most of these registers are dedicated to some purpose, e.g. esp, ebp, ....
For example you are 
inside a loop so you are using ecx for the counter and you execute div the
result will be placed in
eax and the remainder is placed in edx. Typically eax, ecx, edx, esi, edi
are the only free registers
at best and in this case three are being constantly over written. This is
not the case on the Alpha
since you have 4 times as many.

FPU instructions are more cumbersome on the Intel since are operations
involve the sp(0). This
is not so on the Alpha. All registers, with the exception of $FP31 which is
always set to 0.0, can
be used interchangeably. The FPU is pipelined and can simultaneously
execute 2 instructions 
per clock cycle. Only the Pentium Pro has 2 FPUs and can pipeline.
Unfortunately it shares the
same draw backs as the other Intel processors.

Another advantage of the Alpha is it forces good code (if that is possble),
well better code. The 
FPU will generate exceptions for invalid or nonsense instructions rather
than simply carrying on
as if nothing has happened.

