> gcc/g77 for the Alpha so that we can really use Linux instead.  I
> would love to now, but I can't.
>
> > The Alpha has much better exception handling than the PII. The PII is
> > setting the register to INF upon division
> > by zero and continues processing. To me the Alpha is correct. You have
> > asked the processor to do something
> > which does not make sense and it has reported an error. Whereas the PII has
> > returned an answer which has to
> > interpreted as being an invalid answer. A lot of sloppy mathematical code
> > will run fine on an Intel chip producing
> > a result which really has no meaning. This is not so on an Alpha. It is
> > better to get no answer than an answer
> > which is spurious and can be wrongly interpreted as correct. Hence write
> > and develop your code on the alpha this way it
> > will be thoroughly tested. There are instructions in AXP which allow you to
> > specifiy a function to be called upon
> > a floating point exception and have the function handle the error. So it is
> > possible to get the same result, although
> > I would not want to.
> >
> > This is one of the main reasons for complications for poorly written
> > mathematics code to not be easily ported from
> > Intel to Alpha.
> >
> > Conrad Sanderson <conrad@hive.me.gu.edu.au> wrote in article
> > <slrn737pv0.5bf.conrad@hive.me.gu.edu.au>...
