Just thought to add a couple of lines concerning the Pentium Pro.
I'm no expert but I've read in the Intel manuals that the PPro
is actually a RISC machine with a x86 decoder. Internally, it has
many registers ( I think that was 32 general purpose registers and
some more for the FPU, or something close enough ) that get reassigned
dynamically as needed. This means that you can still only program with
8 registers but the CPU is stashing those awayin it's "real" ones and
thus can do more operations simultaneously. That's one reason why they
are so much faster than "regular" Pentiums.

Jean-Francois Landry (jf.landry@vif.com)



