  charlet@innova.nouce (Charles E. Taylor) wrote:

> > I would, but the install crashes, so I can't install it...
>
> Is there any version of Redhat that *doesn't* crash on install with an
> Alpha?  I had to beat on 5.1 to get it installed.  I think now I'll
> leave it on rather than jump immediately on the upgrade train.

Well... there is Hard Hat (TM) which is a derivative of Red Hat, fixed and
optimised specifically for Alpha.

From Hard Data Ltd.
http://www.harddata.com

-------

There are the following limitations on the UX with Linux:

1) Due to the current software PALcode being used the OS ( kernel, libs,
etc.) must be located in the first 2GB of your disk space. Not a big deal..

2) The current libraries used in Red Hat 5.1 must be patched ( 3 of them)
if you are using more than 256MB of RAM.

Also bear in mind that Red Hat has traditionally been very sloppy in their
Alpha CPU release versions. We build systems to run Linux, and due to the
messy nature of Red Hat have been forced to do our own modified
distribution of Red Hat. We call it Hard Hat Linux.

While based on Red Hat, it incorporates the approximately 190 patch rpms
released since the onset of 5.1, plus about 45 patches and updates from
other sources, plus about 30 changes we wrote. Added to this is a number of
applications that our customers have requested, and we we end up with
something so far differing from Red Hat it would have taken another CDROM
set to allow our customers to incorporate the changes if they wanted to
re-install their system. So we produce our own CDROMs now. As the average
user on the newsgroups and mail lists is asking for help with a stock ( or
nearly so) Red Hat install it is not entirely suprising that some people
are having a difficult time with it..

Finally, The Red Hat distribution is a "catch-all" designed to work with
a number of verisons of Alpha machines, with a wide range of CPU models,
chip sets, video cards, SCSI controllers, and so on. As most of these are
not current, except in used or surplus equipment, and as we are focusing
specifically on the current chipsets and motherboards, and modern
peripherals, we have focused on the current components that we are using in
our systems, and tailored the distribution to support these items.

One area that can be complex and rapidly changing is libraries and
compilers, and we spend extra time and attention in keeping these both
"state-of-the-art" and performing properly.

---------

They are working on 5.2 fixes.

CJ

