X-Deja-AN: 408813587
References: <Pine.LNX.3.96.981031011401.1856A-100000@lemur.phy.duke.edu> <slrn73qihg.dp0.conrad@hive.me.gu.edu.au> <363F3118.7A28BB64@nist.gov> <3640478F.4F2C8020@theo.physik.uni-stuttgart.de>
Organization: Moene Computational Physics, Maartensdijk, The Netherlands
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

"C.Simmendinger" <chrisi@theo.physik.uni-stuttgart.de> wrote:

> The only thing which is sort of sad about egcs ist the absolutely poor
> performance of g77 with respect to complex numbers.
> I'm just trying to optimize the BLAS a bit more, i.e. just wrote
> a faster cgemv/zgemv. Had to do all calculations in floats
> with the corresponding conversions complex->float, float->complex.
> In doing so i got a speedup of *4-6*.
> I just keep wondering why the performace is so
> embarrassing with respect to this ?
>  Since a lot of numerical stuff (e.g LAPACK) is written in fortran,
> improvements on this sector would help quite a bit.

The cause is not exactly known - it is related to the way complex numbers are 
treated in the "middle-end" of the compiler, i.e. as a (real, imag) pair.  On 
64-bit hosts (Alpha, MIPS-4000 and higher, UltraSPARC, etc.) a COMPLEX number 
is a 2x32 bits entity, which (for optimisation purposes) sometimes is treated 
as a single 64 bit quantity.  When storing/retrieving this from memory that's 
not a problem - however, when trying to stuff a COMPLEX number in a 64 bit 
register, you're asking for trouble.

The corollary of this is that for DOUBLE COMPLEX, this shouldn't be an issue.  
Unfortunately, although I haven't seen evidence of the contrary, I cannot 
proof this.

HTH,

