Alex, I believe I can handle this one.

Alex wrote:

> After reading this newsgroup and browsing the various alpha
> web sites, I'm still confused regarding the type memory
> my soon to arrive AlphaStation 200 233Mhz (from webauction)
> can use.

> Is "parity memory" mean the same as ECC memory, like I use
> on my PC-AT Intel-Endeavor Pentium motherboard,
> or is it the parity memory that IBM PS/2 computers use?
>

Think pre-Pentium.  The AlphaStation 200 is a discontinued "mature"
product from Digital. It was designed in the days when RAM needed a
means of error checking.  Thus the use of what is called "parity
memory."

Unless the SIMMs you order indicate specifically that they meet this
parity requirement you will experience drastic problems with your
system ranging from a "Parity error. System halted." message in the
middle of doing something important, to failure to boot.  I
experienced both at one time on a 25MHz 386 system which received the
wrong type of RAM.

ECC, and EDO RAM are both non-parity.  They're both newer technology.
They both use an 8 bit addressing/storage system.  Parity RAM uses a 9
bit system in which the 9th bit is used for error/parity checking.

>
> What is the physical configuration of the SIMMs?
> Does a 16Mb SIMM have 9 "big" chips, and 4 "smaller" chips on it?
>

This varies by both manufacturer and specific product.  Kingston, at
one time, made 16MB SIMMs with at least 2 different configurations.

>
> Is there a marking on the SIMMs that ends in   x32,  x36, or x40 ?
>

Usually not.  The 64MB 16X36 72pin parity SIMMS I just installed had
no such markings.  In fact, aside from identifying pin numbers and a
manufacturers internal ID number the only identifying marks were on
the individual chips.  Those markings were merely a catalog number for
that particular chip type, and an indication that they were
manufactured in Korea.

>
> Thanks,
>
> Alex

The cheapest source I've found to date for the 72 pin Parity SIMMs you
need is in San Jose, CA.  Link to Memory World
<http://www.chipmerchant.com> for details.  I just acquired two 64MB
72 pin Parity SIMMs for $137.00 each plus shipping & handling
($10.00).  The next lowest price I've seen is upwards of $150 per
stick.

Keith
