In article <3642F299.E5674C5F@po.cwru.edu>,
D. Keith Higgs <dkh2@po.cwru.edu> wrote:
>> Is "parity memory" mean the same as ECC memory, like I use
>> on my PC-AT Intel-Endeavor Pentium motherboard,
>> or is it the parity memory that IBM PS/2 computers use?
>
>ECC, and EDO RAM are both non-parity.  They're both newer technology.
>They both use an 8 bit addressing/storage system.  Parity RAM uses a 9
>bit system in which the 9th bit is used for error/parity checking.
>
ECC is definitely parity memory.  ECC stands for Error Correcting Codes
8x32 has no parity bits, each address has 8 bits of storage
8x36 has 1 parity bit, each address has 9 bits, the 1 extra bit 
   is checked against the sum of the other 8 bits, thus single
   bit errors can be Detected.
8x40 (ECC) has 2 parity bits, each address has 10 bits, the 2 extra bits
   are used with Hamming error correcting tables to Correct
   single bit errors and Detect two bit errors 
   ECC is generally only used on Server-Class PCs, but its common
