Pavel,

A few considerations:

1) The Alpha 21264 will be available in January from the 3rd party clone
makers. It will be a really big step forward in performance and the gap
between the Alpha and Intel will be really wide at that point.
   I'd be curious to know whether anyone posting here has a good sense of
just how 21264 will compare with 21164 and Intel PII processors in actual
use.

2) You can run the general run-of-the-mill Win32 Intel apps on Alpha NT.
Yes, they are running in a kind of simulation with a compiler that
translates Intel assembly to Alpha assembly. Yes, there is a performance
penalty.
   Also, there are native versions of a few Microsoft apps like Word and
Excel. I'd expect their performance on Alpha NT to be pretty good but I have
no personal experience to compare. Last time I checked there was no native
Alpha Win32 MS Access (which is a bummer since it is the one shrinkwrap
office automation app that I use in a very CPU-intensive manner - almost
everything else I do is compiled by me and I could make native Alpha
versions myself if need be).

3) My guess is that MSVC will do actual compilation faster on the same
hardware than will the Gnu compilers. I've read posts of people who claim
that various commercial PC compilers are 3 to 6 times faster than the
freeware compilers in doing the actual compiles.
   So if you run NT on Intel or Alpha my guess is that your compile time
will be faster. Whether that is important to you depends on how much time
you spend compiling versus running programs.

4) On Alpha last time I read about this several months ago the freeware
compilers produced floating point code that was considerably slower than the
commercial compilers. DEC's Unix C compiler produced code that ran faster
than MSVC's which, in turn, ran faster than egcc and/or other Gnu compiled
code.

5) On Intel NT you can get a compiler from Intel that will plug into the
MSVC environment that will produce faster floating point code for Intel NT
than MSVC's own compiler does. Intel also has some fast math libraries
available for download. I have a friend who uses the Intel compiler and
libraries and is quite pleased with the performance advantage he's thereby
gained.

I'd like to hear from anyone here who has fairly recent comparative
experiences with various compilers and CPUs and perhaps has a more recent
and more accurate perspective on some of the points I have raised here.


